# Defect 355, panel 192's R7, 2026-10-05: an `assert`'s sides go through the
# runtime's one printer too, so a side holding a control character, a
# bidirectional control or a NUL is written by its code and whole, where
# `%s` sent the ESC to the terminal and stopped at the NUL. The override is
# written by its code because it would reorder the line the terminal draws.
# Panel 230's R3, 2026-10-26: two `str` sides are now written as values,
# quoted in the specification's escapes (`\u{1b}`, and `\u{0}` for the NUL, the
# lldb formatter's spelling), so a side reads as the literal it is; a message
# keeps the `<U+001B>` form, as the `.must()` case beside this one does.

function main()
    b: [u8] = [97, 0, 98]
    x = "a\u{1b}[2J\u{202e}b\u{85}c" + b.validated_bytes().must()
    assert x == "y"
